Watercolor paintings are created using water-soluble pigments applied with water onto paper or other suitable surfaces. This medium is characterized by its transparent appearance, delicate color transitions, and fluid painting effects. Artists commonly use techniques such as washes, layering, and wet-on-wet applications to produce variations in color intensity, texture, and tonal depth.
